# Define a public-safe reconstruction algorithm gate with bounded similarity

Treat the maintainer's complete lawful installation as the internal 100%
reference for coverage analysis, but do not generate the distributable algorithm
directly from that complete installation and do not publish an artifact that
embeds or reconstructs it without the user's lawful local source. Treat roughly
60% as the current planning estimate for a possible minimum installation and
45–55% as the tentative candidate-similarity window; validate both concepts
empirically before code uses them.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Specify what “100% installation”, “minimum installation”, and “similarity”
  mean in measurable, content-safe terms before choosing a production metric;
  treat the current ~60% minimum-installation estimate as planning only.
- [ ] Do not author a distributable `algorithm/*.txt` plan by serializing,
  full-tree diffing, or otherwise turning the complete 100% reference
  installation into direct recipe material.
- [ ] Keep the original user installation read-only throughout validation and
  reconstruction; algorithm execution writes only to SHAR-owned output/cache.
- [ ] Evaluate the tentative 45–55% similarity window; do not hardcode it until
  false-positive and false-negative evidence exists across lawful installs.
- [ ] Ensure passing similarity can never replace required local source bytes,
  exact-file gates, hashes, structural validation, or cryptographic/provenance
  checks.
- [ ] Ensure distributable family plans under `algorithm/*.txt` use the
  implemented `shar.algorithm.v1` source-bound contract and never become a
  reversible encoding of the maintainer's private complete installation.
- [ ] Keep plan-contained hashes limited to ordinary exact-source binding and
  protected target verification. Projected variant source records must not carry
  a source hash. No plan metadata is an antivirus verdict or replacement for
  caller-supplied source bytes.
- [ ] Document the finalized metric and limitations in README when implemented;
  do not describe the tentative percentage as a legal safe harbor.

## Dependencies

Depends on representative source-installation evidence and final product
admission integration. The generic `shar.algorithm.v1` serialization mechanism
already exists and is not itself a similarity threshold.

## Evidence

`docs/technical/security/public-safe-reconstruction-gate.md` now defines the
100% reference, minimum installation, reference coverage, and a candidate
weighted-Jaccard similarity metric in content-safe count-vector terms. The
private reference contributes aggregate obfuscated directory/extension counts,
not payload bytes, file names, or a reversible full-tree recipe.

`tools/source-similarity/adapter-inbound/main.py` implements only evidence
measurement using exact rational arithmetic. Its command-line boundary reads two
public-safe JSONL count ledgers, emits exact coverage/Jaccard fractions without
an admission result, and keeps malformed-input diagnostics free of caller paths.
Its regression suite covers complete, missing, extra, invalid,
collision-disambiguated, and non-generated suffix vectors. The companion
`observe-manifest-counts` adapter now derives those rows read-only from a lawful
local source, validates the same exact required root evidence as manifest
generation, and keeps private source paths out of failures. Ephemeral
observations use `count` rather than the minimum-policy ledger's `min`, and the
similarity parser rejects rows or whole ledgers that mix those meanings. It also
requires directory coordinates to retain the manifest producer's lowercase,
forward-slash normalization (while preserving the empty game-root alias), so
case or separator drift cannot turn equivalent structure into false
dissimilarity. Calibration aliases now also retain the manifest producer's
exact two-endpoint lowercase shape after collision normalization. The current
Rust lowercase table has only one two-scalar expansion, so impossible 3/4-scalar
aliases and short
readable private names cannot be accepted as supposedly obfuscated evidence.
Alias components also retain the source tree's portable-host character policy,
so controls, invisible modifiers, reserved punctuation, and trailing dot/space
identities cannot enter calibration after obfuscation. Direct programmatic count
vectors are also captured once before validation and measurement, reject
coordinates outside the producer's classified bucket taxonomy or complete
collision-family shape, and prevent a stateful mapping from presenting different
counts to those two phases.
JSONL calibration rows now also require the producer's compact JSON token
framing and literal member names, rejecting all JSON token whitespace or
escaped keys while preserving valid decoded JSON string-value escapes. Optional
public
metadata distinguishes omission from explicit
JSON `null`;
excessively nested JSON, producer-impossible signed-zero tokens, zero-valued
observation `count` rows, and ledgers without coordinate rows also fail through
the calibrator's path-free input error; zero policy `min` rows remain valid
optional coordinates. The generated game-root `png` policy coordinate is pinned
to zero because source observation explicitly excludes that generated artifact
bucket. Present row
kinds must
belong to the producer's public taxonomy and match the
producer's exact `(dir, ext)` bucket classification, while unclassifiable
coordinates fail even when `kind` is omitted. JSON object members and coordinate
rows must also retain the producer's deterministic field and `(dir, ext)` order.
Taxonomy and required-file
lists, when present, must retain the producer's exact canonical values and
order;
required-file metadata must also remain a portable source-relative path under
the shared host-safe component rules. Reserved aliases, stream syntax,
trailing-dot/space identities, control
characters, invisible path modifiers, and oversized components all fail closed.
Required-file rows are additionally limited to the schema-v2 public path/minimum
pairs, with a regression that parses the tracked public manifest so producer and
calibrator metadata cannot drift independently; duplicate required-file rows
also fail closed. These ignored metadata fields
therefore cannot smuggle arbitrary private
path or kind strings into an accepted
calibration ledger. Ledger paths must also identify non-redirected regular files
beneath non-redirected lexical directory parents before bytes are read,
preventing calibration from traversing symlinks or Windows junctions or treating
other special filesystem nodes as JSONL. Calibration reads one open file
snapshot and requires its device, inode, modification time, platform `ctime`,
and size to remain stable through an immediate opened-path check, the read,
and the final path check. Descriptor/current-path parity rejects redirects and
replacements; `ctime` adds drift evidence on hosts where it tracks metadata
changes. The read-only observation
adapter now fails when an exact-file-only source has
no countable structural rows, so success cannot produce an empty unusable
ledger.
Generated manifest collision ordinals are aggregated into their structural
family before comparison because a partial candidate can legitimately change
those ordinals. Parsed ledgers now require each suffixed family to contain the
producer's complete contiguous `~01..~NN` sequence with at least two members and
reject a simultaneous base alias without a suffix, so malformed orphan/gapped
ordinals
cannot be normalized into apparently valid evidence. Hard manifest and deep
validation remain exact and unchanged. No 45–55%
threshold exists in that code. The technical contract also pins the read-only
source boundary, source-bound plan publication restrictions, and the rule that
similarity cannot substitute for any hard source gate.

Repository publication policy now parses the substantive tracked icon plan and
every non-empty public family `algorithm/*.txt` plan, requiring the exact
`shar.algorithm.v1` top-level source/target structure, lowercase SHA-256 source
bindings, 96-bit nonce encoding, and non-empty hexadecimal protected payloads.
The current icon plan satisfies that guard with one source descriptor and seven
protected targets. The three platform game-family files remain deliberate empty
placeholders and are therefore not claimed as validated release plans. The
publication guard also requires substantive plan paths to be real repository
files rather than symbolic links, so external content cannot satisfy validation
while Git publishes only a redirect. The same guard now mirrors key replay
constraints: a real active settings file with Rust-equivalent schema and
resource validation, its hash in Rust's fixed settings wire order, integer wire
types, duplicate-key rejection, bounded integer/resource limits,
protected-payload tag length,
source/target portable relative-path policy, deterministic source-record
grouping, and case-equivalent target ancestor collisions.
This structural guard narrows what can be committed publicly; it does not
replace
source-backed cryptographic replay evidence or the pending similarity
calibration. It also mirrors the runtime's single projected-source limit, so a
plan with multiple otherwise valid projected inputs fails repository policy
before publication instead of becoming a runtime-only rejection. Algorithm
authoring enforces that same limit before writing a plan, preventing the creator
from publishing a document that its own replay path would reject.

A separate maintainer-only `source-variant-evidence` helper checks whether a
private cross-variant common-byte artifact is an ordered subsequence of supplied
local variants while emitting only argument ordinals and aggregate byte counts.
Its `--projection` mode can also emit an `offset-mask-set-v1` descriptor
without a source hash, containing only bounded positional layouts. It now
rejects more than the generic algorithm contract's 256 distinct alternatives
and binds reference/candidate sizes, spans, and aggregate mask bytes to the
active algorithm file limit before emitting a descriptor that authoring would
reject. Private reference and variant paths now reject symlinked or
junction-backed lexical
directory parents before any payload read. Their read snapshots compare both the
opened descriptor and current lexical path identity before reading bytes and
retain device, inode, modification time, platform `ctime`, and size through the
final path check. Projection derivation grows the packed bit mask only
through the latest selected offset instead of reserving for the full candidate
or retaining one flag byte for every candidate byte. The three
executable variants retained in the current private workspace all reproduce
the full 284,682-byte common sequence and require three distinct layouts. That
result is intentionally limited to those available samples and does not
certify other editions.

The generic algorithm engine now accepts one projected direct-file source record
without a whole-file SHA-256. Authoring binds the protected target to the common
bytes; replay tries only the authenticated plan's bounded layout alternatives
against the caller-supplied file and succeeds only when one projected byte
sequence authenticates the plan. Ordinary source records remain exact-hash
bound. The projection stores no executable payload bytes or whole-file hashes,
and source files remain read-only. Replay retains one source snapshot and
materializes only one projected candidate at a time, so the alternative count
cannot multiply candidate-byte memory before authentication. This closes the
mechanism gap for known multi-edition layouts without turning similarity into
admission evidence; the P0 task remains open for representative-install
calibration and final product policy.

The generic `shar.algorithm.v1` round-trip suite now includes explicit evidence
that a serialized plan cannot replay with zero caller-supplied source roots and
that successful create/replay leaves the caller source bytes and source layout
unchanged. Existing overlap guards continue to reject algorithm output inside a
source root. Authoring additionally rejects exact or nested source/target root
overlap in either containment direction before plan publication, preventing a
caller from turning source payloads into protected target material merely by
pointing the target back at the source tree. Authoring also rejects target files
that are hard-link aliases of any source file by cross-platform physical file
identity, closing a path-disjoint alias of the same payload. Source collection
also rejects repeated physical files before source file/byte limits are checked,
so hard-link aliases cannot inflate generic admission evidence. Target
collection rejects repeated physical files as unsupported hard-link topology
rather than replaying them as independent copies. Replay document validation
also rejects ancestor/descendant target path collisions before
decryption or writes, requiring one realizable protected output tree. Target
paths are compared by the repository's Unicode-uppercase portable identity, so
case-equivalent paths also fail before replay. Algorithm creation uses an atomic
create-new filesystem write, and a regression proves an existing plan remains
byte-identical when publication collides. Replay final files also use create-new
byte persistence, so an output appearing after the absence check cannot be
silently replaced. Generic filesystem failures are
projected as operation plus I/O error kind without caller paths, and a synthetic
missing-source regression proves a private-looking source path is not disclosed.
Authoring captures each admitted source/target file once, and key derivation
plus
target encryption reuse those validated bytes rather than reopening paths during
the operation. These tests establish engine-level source binding and read-only
behavior; they do not certify
the still-placeholder distributable family plans or replace the pending
representative-install similarity calibration.

The ~60% minimum-installation estimate and 45–55% similarity window remain
design targets, not validated production evidence, legal conclusions, or safe
harbors. The task remains open for representative-install calibration and final
algorithm serialization/admission integration.
